- Age: Younger or very young mothers may have higher risk of PPD due to less maturity or support; older mothers might have more stability.
- Husband’s monthly income: Higher income generally correlates with better resources and lower stress, reducing PPD risk.
- Monthly income before latest pregnancy: Higher income before pregnancy may indicate better baseline socioeconomic status, reducing PPD risk.
- Occupation before latest pregnancy: Certain occupations (e.g., student, housewife) might have less social support or more stress affecting PPD risk.
- Marital status: Married status generally provides support, lowering PPD risk; divorced might increase risk.
- Relationship with husband: Poor or bad relationship increases risk of PPD; good or friendly reduces risk.
- Newborn illness: Illness in newborn increases stress and risk of PPD.
- Angry after latest child birth: Feeling angry after childbirth is a strong indicator of PPD.

